    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This thesis examines the origins and evolution of the 1953 Mutual Defense Treaty between the United States and the Republic of Korea (ROK). Contrary to the conventional belief that the Treaty was a direct response to the outbreak of the Korean War (1950-1953), this study uncovers its roots extending back to pre-war discussions in 1948. \r\nFurthermore, this paper highlights the overlooked role of the ROK’s first president, Syngman Rhee, in countering communism in his nascent nation. Rhee’s diplomatic approach to regional security involved simultaneously pursuing three main proposals: forming a “Pacific Pact”, securing a bilateral defense agreement with the U.S., and obtaining a U.S. declaration to defend a unified Korea. The evolution of these proposals amidst geopolitical challenges, such as the Chinese Civil War (1927-1949) and the Korean War, is examined to understand their impact on the eventual formation of the U.S.-ROK Mutual Defense Treaty. This paper contends that the concurrent development and eventual failure of the Pacific Pact substantially shaped the trajectory of the U.S.-ROK Mutual Defense Treaty.\r\nThrough a meticulous examination of primary sources, including diplomatic correspondences, speeches, and memoirs, alongside existing scholarship, this research provides a nuanced understanding of the early Cold War security dynamics in the Asia-Pacific region. By drawing lessons from the Pacific Pact’s failure, this paper offers insights for contemporary regional security cooperation in the Asia-Pacific region.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
